<article class="env-example" id="A16A1E">
  <p><a class="environment-identifier" href="/tag/A16A1E">Example <span data-tag="A16A1E">8.2.9.22</span>.</a> Let $\calJ $ be a Reedy category with $\calJ ^{L} = \calJ $, and let ${\mathbf A}$ be a model category. Then the weak equivalences and cofibrations of the Reedy model structure (Proposition <a href="/tag/EDC2F7" data-tag="EDC2F7">8.2.9.19</a>) are the injective cofibrations and the weak equivalences appearing in Definition <a href="/tag/AECA43" data-tag="AECA43">8.2.8.1</a>. It follows that the Reedy model structure on $\Fun (\calJ , {\mathbf A})$ coincides with the injective model structure of Proposition <a href="/tag/B160E2" data-tag="B160E2">8.2.8.2</a> (in particular, the injective model structure is well defined in this case even without the assumption that ${\mathbf A}$ is combinatorial). Similarly, if $\calJ ^{R} = \calJ $, then we can identify the Reedy model structure on $\Fun (\calJ , {\mathbf A})$ with the projective model structure of Proposition <a href="/tag/B160E2" data-tag="B160E2">8.2.8.2</a>. </p>
  <p>In the general case, we can regard the Reedy model structure on $\Fun (\calJ , {\mathbf A})$ as a mixture of the projective and injective model structures. More precisely, we have the following: </p>
  <p><ul>
  <li class="custom-item" data-marker="$(i)$"><p>
A natural transformation $F \rightarrow G$ in $\Fun (\calJ , {\mathbf A})$ satisfies condition $(C)$ of Proposition <a href="/tag/EDC2F7" data-tag="EDC2F7">8.2.9.19</a> if and only if the induced transformation $F|\calJ ^{R} \rightarrow G| \calJ ^{R}$ is a projective cofibration in $\Fun (\calJ ^{R}, {\mathbf A})$. 
</p>
</li><li class="custom-item" data-marker="$(ii)$"><p>
A natural transformation $F \rightarrow G$ in $\Fun (\calJ , {\mathbf A})$ satisfies condition $(F)$ of Proposition <a href="/tag/EDC2F7" data-tag="EDC2F7">8.2.9.19</a> if and only if the induced transformation $F|\calJ ^{L} \rightarrow G| \calJ ^{L}$ is a injective fibration in $\Fun (\calJ ^{L}, {\mathbf A})$. 
</p>
</li><li class="custom-item" data-marker="$(iii)$"><p>
A natural transformation $F \rightarrow G$ in $\Fun (\calJ , {\mathbf A})$ satisfies condition $(WC)$ of Proposition <a href="/tag/EDC2F7" data-tag="EDC2F7">8.2.9.19</a> if and only if the induced transformation $F|\calJ ^{R} \rightarrow G| \calJ ^{R}$ is a trivial projective cofibration in $\Fun (\calJ ^{R}, {\mathbf A})$. 
</p>
</li><li class="custom-item" data-marker="$(iv)$"><p>
A natural transformation $F \rightarrow G$ in $\Fun (\calJ , {\mathbf A})$ satisfies condition $(WF)$ of Proposition <a href="/tag/EDC2F7" data-tag="EDC2F7">8.2.9.19</a> if and only if the induced transformation $F|\calJ ^{L} \rightarrow G| \calJ ^{L}$ is a trivial injective fibration in $\Fun (\calJ ^{L}, {\mathbf A})$. 
</p>
</li>
</ul></p>
